questions.question:
Given the configuration:
```nginx
location /media/ {
    root /var/data;
}
```
What file path on disk does NGINX look for when a client requests `GET /media/audio/song.mp3`?

Given the configuration:
```nginx
location /media/ {
    alias /var/data/content/;
}
```
What file path on disk does NGINX look for when a client requests `GET /media/audio/song.mp3`?

Given:
```nginx
location /api/ {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8001;
}
```
A client requests `GET /api/v1/stats?page=2`. What request line does the backend receive?

Given:
```nginx
location /api/ {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8001/;
}
```
A client requests `GET /api/v1/stats`. What path does the backend receive?

Given:
```nginx
location /api {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8001/;
}
```
A client requests `GET /api/v1/stats`. What path does the backend receive?

Given:
```nginx
location /api/ {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8001/v2;
}
```
A client requests `GET /api/users`. What path does the backend receive?

Given:
```nginx
location ~ ^/api/(.*)$ {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8001/$1;
}
```
A client requests `GET /api/v1/items?page=2`. What does the backend receive?

Given:
```nginx
upstream lumina_api_nodes { server 127.0.0.1:8001; }
server {
    server_name lumina.local;
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;

    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://lumina_api_nodes;
        proxy_set_header X-Request-ID $request_id;
    }
}
```
What does the backend see for a request to `lumina.local/api/x`?

During a refactor, this:
```nginx
location /api/ { proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8001/; }
```
becomes this:
```nginx
location /api/ {
    set $api_backend 127.0.0.1:8001;
    proxy_pass http://$api_backend/;
}
```
Now every API call returns the backend's home page, and the backend log shows `GET /` for every request. What changed?

Given:
```nginx
upstream api {
    server 127.0.0.1:8001 weight=3;
    server 127.0.0.1:8002;
}
```
About how do 100 sequential requests split?

Given:
```nginx
upstream cache_tier {
    hash $request_uri consistent;
    server 10.0.0.1;
    server 10.0.0.2;
    server 10.0.0.3 backup;
}
```
What happens when you run `nginx -t`?

On **nginx 1.24**, a team writes:
```nginx
upstream api { server 10.0.0.5:8080; keepalive 16; }
location /api/ { proxy_pass http://api; }
```
Do connections to the backend get reused?

Given:
```nginx
proxy_read_timeout 3s;
proxy_next_upstream error timeout;
proxy_next_upstream_timeout 4s;
```
Both backends take 5 s to answer. Roughly when does the client receive its `504`?

Given:
```nginx
location /ws/chat {
    proxy_pass http://chat;
    proxy_set_header Upgrade    $http_upgrade;
    proxy_set_header Connection $connection_upgrade;
    proxy_read_timeout 5s;
}
```
A client completes the WebSocket handshake, exchanges two messages, then sends nothing. Neither side sends pings. What happens?

An NGINX Plus upstream is defined as:
```nginx
upstream api {
    least_conn;
    server 10.0.0.1:8080;
    server 10.0.0.2:8080;
}
```
The API location has `api write=on;`. You `POST` a new server to `/api/10/http/upstreams/api/servers`. What happens?

On NGINX Plus, the management location is configured as:
```nginx
location /api/ {
    api;
}
```
An automation script sends `PATCH /api/10/http/upstreams/api/servers/2` with `{"drain":true}`. What is the result?

On NGINX Plus:
```nginx
upstream api {
    zone api 64k;
    state /var/lib/nginx/state/api.state;
    server 10.0.0.1:8080;
}
```
What does `nginx -t` say?

On **open source** nginx 1.31, someone copies a Plus snippet:
```nginx
upstream api {
    zone api 64k;
    least_conn;
    server 10.0.0.1:8080 slow_start=30s;
}
```
What happens?

On open source nginx 1.31:
```nginx
upstream api {
    zone api 64k;
    resolver 127.0.0.53 valid=5s;
    server api.lumina.test:8001 resolve;
}
```
The DNS record for `api.lumina.test` changes from 127.0.0.1 to 127.0.0.3. What happens?
